    <h2 id="etsy-requirement">Does Etsy Require Sellers to Have a Privacy Policy</h2>

    <p>Yes. Etsy's Seller Policy states that sellers who collect, use, or process personal information from buyers must provide a privacy policy that discloses those practices. While Etsy handles much of the transaction infrastructure (payment processing through Etsy Payments, messaging through the Etsy platform, and order management through the seller dashboard), sellers are still responsible for how they handle the buyer data Etsy shares with them to fulfill orders.</p>

    <p>When a buyer places an order, Etsy provides the seller with the buyer's name, shipping address, email address (in some cases), and order details. That transfer of personal data makes the seller a data controller under laws like the GDPR. Etsy acts as a platform and processes payments on your behalf, but the moment buyer data reaches your end for fulfillment, shipping label creation, customer communication, or marketing, you are responsible for disclosing how you handle it.</p>

    <p>Etsy does not generate or host a privacy policy for sellers. Unlike some e-commerce platforms that offer a built-in privacy policy template, Etsy expects sellers to create and manage their own. The platform provides a designated section in your shop's policies where you can paste a privacy policy, but the content is entirely your responsibility.</p>

    <h2 id="why-needed">Why Etsy Shop Owners Need a Privacy Policy</h2>

    <p>Beyond Etsy's own seller requirements, three forces make a privacy policy effectively mandatory for every Etsy shop, regardless of size.</p>

    <p><strong>Privacy laws apply based on where your buyers are located.</strong> If a buyer in France orders a handmade item from your shop, the GDPR applies to that transaction. A buyer from California triggers the CCPA. Canadian buyers bring PIPEDA into scope, and Brazilian buyers are covered by the LGPD. Each of these laws requires you to publish a clear privacy policy explaining what personal data you collect and why. Since Etsy shops are accessible worldwide, most sellers are subject to multiple privacy regulations simultaneously. GDPR fines for transparency failures have reached tens of millions of euros, and CCPA enforcement has targeted businesses of all sizes, including sole proprietors.</p>

    <p><strong>Third-party services you use outside Etsy require disclosure.</strong> Many Etsy sellers use tools and services beyond what Etsy provides. If you use Google Analytics to track shop traffic, ship through USPS, UPS, or FedEx using a service like ShipStation or Pirate Ship, send marketing emails through Mailchimp or Klaviyo, use bookkeeping tools like QuickBooks or Wave, or run advertising through Facebook, Google, or Pinterest, each of those services processes buyer data. Your privacy policy must disclose which third-party services receive personal information and explain what data they handle.</p>

    <p><strong>Buyers expect transparency before purchasing.</strong> A visible privacy policy signals that you take data handling seriously. For buyers entering payment information or sharing their home address with a seller they have never purchased from before, a privacy policy provides reassurance. Shops without one risk losing sales to competitors who clearly communicate their data practices, especially for higher-value items where buyer trust matters most.</p>

    <p>The same disclosure duties apply to sellers on other platforms: <a href="/resources/shopify-privacy-policy-generator/">a Shopify-powered storefront</a> faces nearly identical buyer-data obligations, even though the settings live in a different dashboard. If you are not sure which specific laws your buyer base triggers, <a href="/tools/privacy-law-checker/">a short tool that checks your platform and audience against applicable privacy regulations</a> can settle that in a few clicks.</p>

    <h2 id="what-to-include">What to Include in an Etsy Shop Privacy Policy</h2>

    <p>An Etsy shop's privacy policy needs to cover the specific data flows that occur when someone buys from your store. For a broader walkthrough of structuring each section, read our <a href="/resources/how-to-write-privacy-policy-for-app/">guide to writing a privacy policy</a>.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Types of personal data collected, including what Etsy shares with you.</strong> When a buyer places an order, Etsy shares the buyer's full name, shipping address, and order details (items, quantities, transaction amount) with you as the seller so you can fulfill the purchase. In some cases Etsy also provides the buyer's email address. If buyers contact you through Etsy Messages — for customization requests, order questions, or pre-purchase inquiries — you receive the message content along with their Etsy username, and any files or photos they attach. If you collect email addresses for a mailing list or use Google Analytics through Etsy's web analytics integration, disclose those separately. List every category of data you receive or collect, whether from Etsy directly, from buyer messages, or through third-party tools.</li>
      <li><strong>Payment processing through Etsy Payments.</strong> Etsy requires most sellers to use Etsy Payments as the sole checkout method. Etsy Payments processes all credit card, debit card, Apple Pay, Google Pay, and gift card transactions. Sellers never receive or store buyer payment credentials — no card numbers, no bank details, no CVVs. Your privacy policy should explain that Etsy Payments handles the full payment transaction on your behalf and that you receive only order-level details (buyer name, shipping address, items, and transaction total) needed for fulfillment. If you also accept PayPal in a region where Etsy permits it, disclose that separately.</li>
      <li><strong>How you use buyer data.</strong> Tie each data type to a specific purpose. You use shipping addresses to send orders. You use buyer names and email addresses to communicate about order status. If you use buyer data for marketing, clearly state that and explain how buyers can opt out. Vague language like "to improve your experience" does not satisfy the GDPR's transparency requirements.</li>
      <li><strong>Third-party services.</strong> Name every external service that receives buyer data. Common examples for Etsy sellers include shipping carriers (USPS, UPS, FedEx, Royal Mail), shipping management tools (ShipStation, Pirate Ship, Shippo), email marketing platforms (Mailchimp, Klaviyo), analytics (Google Analytics), advertising platforms (Facebook Ads, Pinterest Ads, Google Ads), and bookkeeping tools (QuickBooks, Wave, TaxJar). Each must be disclosed.</li>
      <li><strong>Cookies and tracking.</strong> Etsy itself sets cookies for session management and platform functionality. If you have enabled Google Analytics through Etsy's web analytics integration (visible in Shop Manager under Settings &gt; Options &gt; Web Analytics), those set additional tracking cookies. Your policy should explain which cookie categories apply (essential, analytics, marketing) and how buyers can manage preferences through browser settings.</li>
      <li><strong>Etsy Ads and Off-Site Ads.</strong> If you use Etsy Ads, your listings are promoted within Etsy's search results and category pages. Etsy manages the targeting; you set a daily budget through the Advertising section in Shop Manager. More significantly, Etsy's Off-Site Ads program promotes your listings on external platforms including Google, Facebook, Instagram, Pinterest, and Bing. Sellers who earned $10,000 or more in the prior 12 months are automatically enrolled with no opt-out. Even sellers below that threshold are enrolled by default, though they can opt out through Shop Manager under Settings &gt; Off-Site Ads. When Etsy runs off-site ads for your products, it shares listing data (product images, titles, prices) with those advertising networks for targeting and attribution. If a buyer clicks an off-site ad and purchases within 30 days, Etsy attributes the sale and charges you an advertising fee. Your privacy policy should disclose that Etsy may share your listing and shop data with external advertising platforms through the Off-Site Ads program, even if you did not actively choose to participate.</li>
      <li><strong>Buyer rights under applicable laws.</strong> Under the GDPR, buyers can access, correct, delete, and port their data, restrict processing, and withdraw consent. Under the CCPA, California residents can request disclosure of collected data, request deletion, and opt out of the sale of personal information. Note that for data Etsy holds directly, buyers should contact Etsy. For data you hold as a seller, you are responsible for responding to these requests.</li>
      <li><strong>Data retention.</strong> State how long you keep buyer data. Order records are typically retained for tax and accounting compliance (often five to seven years depending on jurisdiction). Marketing email lists are kept until the subscriber unsubscribes. Be specific rather than stating "as long as necessary."</li>
      <li><strong>Contact information.</strong> Provide an email address where buyers can reach you with privacy questions. If you sell to EU buyers, you may need to designate a data controller and provide their contact details.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="how-to-add">How to Add a Privacy Policy to Your Etsy Shop</h2>

    <p>Once you have your privacy policy text ready, adding it to your Etsy shop takes just a few minutes. Etsy provides a dedicated section for shop policies that buyers can access from your shop page.</p>

    <ol class="steps-list">
      <li><strong>Sign in to Etsy and open Shop Manager.</strong> Go to etsy.com, sign in to your seller account, and click the Shop Manager icon (the storefront icon in the top navigation) to access your seller dashboard.</li>
      <li><strong>Open Settings.</strong> In the left sidebar of Shop Manager, click the gear icon labeled <strong>Settings</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Scroll to the Policies heading.</strong> On the Settings page, scroll down to the <strong>Policies</strong> section. Etsy displays separate text fields here: <strong>Privacy policy</strong>, <strong>Returns &amp; exchanges</strong>, <strong>Shipping</strong>, and <strong>Additional policies</strong>. Click into the <strong>Privacy policy</strong> field.</li>
      <li><strong>Paste your privacy policy text.</strong> If there is existing placeholder text, select all and replace it with your full privacy policy. Etsy's policies editor supports basic formatting, so you can structure the text with paragraphs and line breaks.</li>
      <li><strong>Click Save.</strong> Your privacy policy will now be visible to buyers who visit your shop and click the <strong>Policies</strong> tab on your shop page.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Some sellers also add a brief mention of their privacy policy in their shop announcement or in the About section of their shop, with a note directing buyers to the Policies tab for the full document. If you collect email addresses through an external form or newsletter signup outside of Etsy, include a link to your privacy policy near the signup form to meet GDPR transparency requirements at the point of data collection.</p>

    <h2 id="generator">Using BuildPrivacyPolicy's Generator for Etsy Sellers</h2>

    <p>Writing a privacy policy from scratch requires cross-referencing GDPR articles, CCPA disclosure requirements, Etsy's seller obligations, and the data practices of every third-party tool you use. That is a lot of legal surface area for a shop owner focused on making and selling products.</p>

    <p>A generator compresses that work into a guided form. You can <a href="https://buildprivacypolicy.com/">generate a privacy policy tailored to your Etsy shop</a> by answering questions that cover:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Your shop name and a contact email for privacy inquiries</li>
      <li>Which platform your shop runs on (select Web App / PWA for an Etsy shop selling through the web)</li>
      <li>The specific data types your shop collects or receives: buyer names, email addresses, shipping addresses, order details, and any additional information from direct communication</li>
      <li>Which third-party services your shop uses: Google Analytics, shipping carriers, email marketing platforms, advertising tools, and bookkeeping software</li>
      <li>How long you retain data and what security measures you follow</li>
      <li>Where your buyers are located, which determines whether the GDPR, CCPA, PIPEDA, LGPD, or other regulations apply</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The generator produces a privacy policy you can host for free on BuildPrivacyPolicy's servers, download as HTML to paste into Etsy's policies section, or copy as plain text. No signup, no account, no payment. The process takes a few minutes from start to finished policy.</p>

    <p>The output covers each required disclosure section across the GDPR, CCPA, COPPA, PIPEDA, and other major regulations. Unlike generic templates found online, the generated policy reflects the specific services, data types, and legal jurisdictions you selected. It is not a substitute for a lawyer's review if your shop handles sensitive data or sells children's products, but for the majority of Etsy sellers running standard handmade, vintage, or craft supply shops, it produces a document that is specific to your setup and far more accurate than copying a generic template.</p>

    <h2 id="example-policy">Sample Etsy Shop Privacy Policy You Can Copy</h2>

    <p>Below is a complete privacy policy written for a fictional Etsy shop called "[Your Shop Name]." It covers the data flows that Etsy sellers handle by default — order information shared by Etsy, Etsy Payments processing, Etsy Messages, Etsy Ads and Off-Site Ads, and common third-party integrations like Google Analytics and shipping carriers. Copy the entire block, replace the bracketed placeholders with your shop's details, and remove any sections that do not apply to your setup.</p>

    <div class="copy-block">
      <div class="copy-block-header">
        <span class="copy-block-label">Sample Privacy Policy — ready to copy</span>
      </div>
      <div class="copy-block-body" id="sample-policy-text">
<p><strong>Privacy Policy for [Your Shop Name]</strong></p>
<p><em>Last updated: [Date]</em></p>

<p><strong>1. Introduction</strong></p>
<p>[Your Shop Name] ("I," "me," or "my") operates an Etsy shop at [your-shop-url]. This privacy policy explains what personal data I collect when you browse or purchase from my shop, why I collect it, who receives it, how long I keep it, and what rights you have over it. This policy covers only the data I handle as a seller. Etsy has its own privacy policy that governs how the platform collects and uses data at the platform level.</p>

<p><strong>2. Data I Collect</strong></p>
<p><em>Order and fulfillment data.</em> When you place an order, Etsy provides me with your name, shipping address, and order details (items purchased, quantity, and transaction amount) so I can fulfill your purchase. In some cases Etsy also shares your email address.</p>
<p><em>Messages.</em> If you contact me through Etsy Messages — for example, to request a customization, ask about an order, or inquire about a product — I receive the content of those messages along with your Etsy username and any files or photos you attach.</p>
<p><em>Payment information.</em> All payments are processed through Etsy Payments. I do not receive or store your credit card number, bank account details, or other payment credentials. Etsy Payments handles the full payment transaction, and I receive only a payout for completed orders along with the order-level details needed for fulfillment.</p>
<p><em>Automatically collected data.</em> Etsy's platform logs visitor data such as IP addresses, browser type, pages viewed, and session duration. I can view aggregated traffic analytics through the Stats section in Shop Manager, but I do not receive individual-level browsing data unless you make a purchase or contact me.</p>
<p>[If you have Google Analytics enabled:] <em>Analytics.</em> I use Google Analytics through Etsy's web analytics integration (configured in Shop Manager under Settings &gt; Options &gt; Web Analytics) to understand traffic patterns and how visitors find my shop. Google Analytics sets cookies (<code>_ga</code>, <code>_ga_[container]</code>) to distinguish unique visitors and track sessions.</p>

<p><strong>3. How I Use Your Data</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Fulfill and ship your orders, print shipping labels, and send order updates</li>
<li>Respond to your messages and handle customer support requests</li>
<li>Comply with tax and accounting obligations by retaining order records</li>
<li>[If applicable:] Send marketing emails if you have opted in through [Mailchimp / Klaviyo / other service]</li>
<li>[If applicable:] Analyze shop traffic through Google Analytics to improve product offerings</li>
</ul>
<p>I do not sell your personal data to third parties.</p>

<p><strong>4. Third Parties That Receive Your Data</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Etsy, Inc.</strong> Hosts my shop, processes payments through Etsy Payments, provides the messaging system, runs shop analytics, and handles buyer-seller dispute resolution. Etsy acts as the platform and has its own privacy policy governing its data practices.</li>
<li><strong>Etsy Ads.</strong> [If applicable:] I use Etsy Ads to promote my listings within Etsy search results and category pages. Etsy manages targeting and display; I set a daily budget through the Advertising section in Shop Manager but do not receive individual ad viewer data.</li>
<li><strong>Etsy Off-Site Ads.</strong> Etsy may advertise my listings on Google, Facebook, Instagram, Pinterest, and Bing through its Off-Site Ads program. If you click an off-site ad and make a purchase within 30 days, Etsy attributes the sale. Etsy shares listing data with these advertising platforms for targeting and attribution. I do not control which platforms Etsy uses or what data it shares with them for off-site advertising. [If you have opted out and are eligible to do so, remove this section.]</li>
<li><strong>Shipping carriers.</strong> [USPS / UPS / FedEx / Royal Mail / your carrier] receives your name and shipping address to deliver your order. [If applicable:] I use [ShipStation / Pirate Ship / Shippo] to generate shipping labels, which means your name and address are also processed by that service.</li>
<li>[If applicable:] <strong>[Mailchimp / Klaviyo].</strong> Stores subscriber email addresses and sends marketing emails. Tracks email opens and link clicks to measure campaign performance.</li>
<li>[If applicable:] <strong>[QuickBooks / Wave / TaxJar].</strong> Receives order totals, dates, and basic buyer information for bookkeeping and tax reporting.</li>
</ul>
<p>I do not share your personal data with any parties beyond those listed above.</p>

<p><strong>5. Cookies and Tracking</strong></p>
<p>Etsy sets its own cookies for session management, cart persistence, and platform functionality. [If applicable:] I have enabled Google Analytics through Etsy's web analytics integration, which sets additional analytics cookies. You can manage cookies through your browser settings; disabling certain cookies may affect Etsy's functionality.</p>

<p><strong>6. Data Retention</strong></p>
<p>Order records (name, address, items purchased, transaction amount) are retained for [7] years to comply with tax and accounting regulations. Message history through Etsy Messages is retained for as long as my shop is active. [If applicable:] Marketing email subscriber data is retained until you unsubscribe, at which point your email is removed from active lists within [7] days. When data is no longer required, I delete or anonymize it.</p>

<p><strong>7. Your Rights Under GDPR</strong></p>
<p>If you are in the European Economic Area or the United Kingdom, you have the right to: access the personal data I hold about you; request correction of inaccurate data; request deletion; object to or restrict processing; request data portability; and withdraw consent at any time. For data Etsy holds directly, contact Etsy through its Help Center. For data I hold as a seller, contact me at [your-email@example.com]. I will respond within 30 days.</p>

<p><strong>8. Your Rights Under CCPA</strong></p>
<p>If you are a California resident, you have the right to: know what personal information I collect and how it is used; request deletion of your personal information; and opt out of the sale of personal information. I do not sell personal information. To submit a request, contact me at [your-email@example.com].</p>

<p><strong>9. Security</strong></p>
<p>Etsy provides SSL/TLS encryption across the platform and handles payment security through Etsy Payments. I do not store payment credentials. I protect my Etsy account with a strong password and two-step verification. While no method of electronic transmission is completely secure, I implement reasonable safeguards to protect your data.</p>

<p><strong>10. Changes to This Policy</strong></p>
<p>I may update this privacy policy from time to time. When I make changes, I will update the "Last updated" date at the top of this page.</p>

<p><strong>11. Contact</strong></p>
<p>If you have questions about this privacy policy or your data, contact me at:<br />[your-email@example.com]<br />[Your Shop Name]</p>
      </div>
